  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn organized MCS prompted Severe Thunderstorm Watch 648 which \nremains in effect until 7PM. This complex is tracking southeast \nalong a stalled frontal boundary north of a subtropical air mass. \nSfc dew points south of the boundary sit in the lower to mid 70s, \nwith steep mid-level lapse rates fueling 2000 to 3000 J/kg of \nSBCAPE. 35 to 40 knots of effective shear has been more than \nsufficient for bowing segments and transient embedded supercellular \nstructures. A few stronger updrafts will have the potential to \nproduce large hail, but the current storm mode favors wind gusts of \n60 to 75 mph as the primary threat. A tornado or two also can't be \nruled out, especially where any segments deviate S to SSE to \ncapitalize on enhanced SRH that exists near Metro Detroit.\n\nHeavy rainfall and localized flooding will be a threat through the \nevent as PWAT sits 2 inches and a deep warm cloud layer depth to 12-\n13 kft supports efficient rainfall processes. While most convection \nhas been relatively progressive which limits residence time of \nheavier rates, the environment supports rates of 1 to 2 inches per \nhour. Corfidi vector magnitude weakens to 10 kt suggesting potential \nfor backbuilding and training convection that may produce pockets of \nrainfall in excess of 2 inches within a few hours. If this occurs \nover urban areas and/or locations that have received heavy rain in \nthe past 24 hours, flooding will be a concern. After the complex \nexits, a period of subsidence supports a lull through the late \nevening with potential for fog development overnight.\n\nThe next shortwave spills over the upstream ridge axis overnight \nbefore passing over Lower MI between 09z and 18z Friday. This wave \npulls the nearby reservoir of higher theta-e back NE and toward the \narea, tightening the instability gradient in place. This offers a \nfavored corridor for nocturnal convection to go upscale and track \nESE through the early to mid morning, with a veering LLJ supplying a \nfeed of subtropical moisture into the elevated frontal boundary. \nShould an organized cold pool capable of overcoming developing CIN \nemerge upstream, available hi-res ML guidance does support potential \nfor isolated to scattered strong to severe wind gusts. Given \nunfavorable diurnal timing and limited surface-based instability, \nthe favored scenario is for a weakening trend and relatively muted \nsevere potential. The environment remains supportive of localized \ntorrential rainfall with any convection overnight into Friday \nmorning.\n\nThe frontal zone migrates slightly southward on Friday, its \nplacement and the subsequent orientation of the instability axis \ndictating where additional convection may become favored by Friday \nafternoon and evening. A majority of CAMs place this corridor across \nwestern Lower MI, intersecting areas as far north as M-59. A similar \nenvironment in place offers potential for another round of strong to \nsevere storms if convection materializes. The SWODY2 highlights a \nmarginal risk for severe weather tomorrow with support from hi-res \nML guidance.\n\nThe upper air pattern evolves into a large scale omega block by \nSaturday, likely holding in place through the weekend with an \namplified ridge extending from Texas into the northern Plains. Our \nplacement within the northwest flow downstream of the ridge axis \nkeeps some potential for showers/storms in the south on Saturday, \nbut expansive high pressure is then shown to dominate the Great \nLakes by the latter half of the weekend and into the early work \nweek. This will bring the return of a more seasonable air mass with \nlower humidity.",
